- What metrics would you track to improve module selection over time?

## Why This Is Asked
Designing a modular, policy-driven prompt assembly system tests understanding of multi-tenant safety, latency budgets, and deterministic prompt composition under constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Fragment catalogs with metadata: policy, latency, locale, tone
- Tenant-aware scoring and ordering under latency limits
- Guardrails and watermark injection when needed
- Audit logging with fragment IDs and decision trace

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal prototype outline (not full implementation)
const fragments = [
  {id:'intent', text:'Explain concisely', latency:10},
  {id:'tone', text:'in a formal style', latency:5},
  {id:'locale', text:'English', latency:2}
];
function assemble(tenantPolicy, budget){
  // simplistic greedy selection
  let chosen = [];
  let used= new Set();
  for(const f of fragments){
    if(!used.has(f.id) && budget- f.latency >=0 && tenantPolicy.includes(f.id)){
      chosen.push(f); used.add(f.id);
      budget -= f.latency;
    }
    if(chosen.length>=3) break;
  }
  return chosen.map(x=>x.text).join(' ');
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle non-determinism in fragment selection across model updates?
- How to validate audits for compliance and GDPR logging obligations?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design a configurable policy framework that adapts prompts based on language and content risk, with traceable moderation reasoning and backpressure.

## Key Concepts
- Language detection and content classification
- Policy routing matrix (per-language, per-category)
- Guardrails and re-prompt loops
- Audit logging for moderation workflows

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal prototype: routePrompt(prompt, lang, category) -> {policy, rePrompt}
function routePrompt(prompt, lang, category){
  const policyKey = (category in policyMap && lang in policyMap[category]) ? policyMap[category][lang] : 'default';
  let rePrompt = null;
  const risk = scoreRisk(prompt, category, lang);
  if(risk > threshold){
    rePrompt = `Please comply with ${policyKey} before proceeding.`;
  }
  return {policy: policyKey, rePrompt};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test cross-language policy consistency?
- How would you store and audit re-prompt decisions at scale?
